"use strict";(self.webpackChunkmy_site=self.webpackChunkmy_site||[]).push([["7358"],{1895(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"/Differential Geometry and Fibre Bundle/1_2","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/Differential Geometry and Fibre Bundle/1_2","source":"@site/blog/Differential Geometry and Fibre Bundle/1_2.mdx","title":"Linear Algebra for Differential Geometry","description":"We assume the reader already has some basic familiarity with linear algebra, such as the definitions of vector spaces and bases.","date":"2026-05-18T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Differential Geometry","permalink":"/blog/tags/differential-geometry"},{"inline":true,"label":"Linear Algebra","permalink":"/blog/tags/linear-algebra"}],"readingTime":5.77,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Linear Algebra for Differential Geometry","date":"2026-05-18T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Differential Geometry","Linear Algebra"]},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"Basic Topology for Differential Geometry","permalink":"/blog/Differential Geometry and Fibre Bundle/1_1"}},"content":"We assume the reader already has some basic familiarity with linear algebra, such as the definitions of vector spaces and bases.\\n\\nThere are several concepts that may not appear in an introductory linear algebra course. For those, I will state the basic definitions here. For more detailed discussions, one may consult *Linear Algebra Done Right* or any standard advanced linear algebra textbook.\\n\\nReaders may notice that some notation conventions differ from those used in other areas. In most cases they refer to exactly the same mathematical objects, so there is no reason to panic.\\n\\n## Vector Spaces\\n\\nIn linear algebra, every finite-dimensional vector space admits a basis $\\\\{e_\\\\mu\\\\}$, such that any vector can be expanded with respect to this basis.\\n\\n:::note[Index conventions]\\n\\nReaders may be more familiar with using $i,j,k$ as indices, or explicitly writing $1,2,3,\\\\dots$.\\n\\nIn differential geometry, Greek letters $\\\\mu,\\\\nu,\\\\rho,\\\\dots$ are typically used for vectors and their components. This is purely a convention.\\n\\nIn the context of general relativity (four-dimensional spacetime), one usually assumes:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\mu,\\\\nu,\\\\rho,\\\\dots = 0,1,2,3,\\n$$\\n\\nwhere $0$ denotes the time component.\\n\\nMeanwhile, due to historical convention, one often writes\\n\\n$$\\ni,j,k,\\\\dots = 1,2,3\\n$$\\n\\nfor spatial indices.\\n\\nOrdinary Roman letters such as $a,b,c$ are often used as abstract indices. They do not take numerical values, but only indicate tensor type. This is a somewhat subtle topic which we will discuss later.\\n\\n:::\\n\\nOnce a basis is chosen, any vector can be expanded as\\n\\n$$\\nv=\\\\sum_\\\\mu v^\\\\mu e_\\\\mu.\\n$$\\n\\nNotice that vector components are written with upper indices here. This is a common convention in differential geometry, and later we will see that it is actually quite natural.\\n\\n### Einstein Summation Convention\\n\\nSo far everything is just standard linear algebra.\\n\\nNow we begin preparing for differential geometry. In differential geometry, expressions with four or more summation signs frequently appear (for example, the full contraction of the Riemann curvature tensor into the Ricci scalar). Writing out every summation sign quickly becomes visually exhausting.\\n\\nTo avoid this, we introduce the **Einstein summation convention**.\\n\\n:::tip[Einstein summation convention]\\n\\nWhenever an upper and lower index with the same symbol appears repeatedly, summation over that index is implied.\\n\\nFor example,\\n\\n$$\\nv^\\\\mu e_\\\\mu = \\\\sum_\\\\mu v^\\\\mu e_\\\\mu,\\n$$\\n\\nor\\n\\n$$\\nA^\\\\mu{}_\\\\nu v^\\\\nu \\\\equiv \\\\sum_\\\\nu A^\\\\mu{}_\\\\nu v^\\\\nu.\\n$$\\n\\nAt the moment, we do not need to worry too much about what all these symbols mean. Their meaning will become clearer later.\\n\\nFor now, one may simply view this as a symbolic rule.\\n\\n:::\\n\\n:::note\\n\\nPhysicists often suppress the basis entirely and define vectors directly through their components $v^\\\\mu$, together with transformation laws.\\n\\nThis approach is often more intuitive, but somewhat less structural.\\n\\nHere we adopt a more mathematical viewpoint, which is useful when discussing invariants and canonical objects.\\n\\nIn particular, we emphasize:\\n\\n1. $v^\\\\mu$ are the components of the vector $v$ in the basis $\\\\{e_\\\\mu\\\\}$;\\n2. $\\\\{e_\\\\mu\\\\}$ are actual vectors.\\n\\n:::\\n\\n## Dual Vector Spaces\\n\\n### Definition\\n\\nWe first define dual vectors.\\n\\n::::tip[Dual vector]\\n\\nA dual vector is a linear functional defined on a vector space $V$:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\omega: V\\\\to\\\\mathbb{R},\\n\\\\qquad\\nv\\\\mapsto\\\\omega(v).\\n$$\\n\\nIt satisfies linearity:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\omega(\\\\alpha v+\\\\beta w)\\n=\\n\\\\alpha\\\\omega(v)+\\\\beta\\\\omega(w),\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\forall v,w\\\\in V.\\n$$\\n\\n:::note\\n\\nIf you are more comfortable with matrix language, you may think of this as a row vector.\\n\\n:::\\n\\n::::\\n\\nAddition and scalar multiplication are naturally defined for dual vectors:\\n\\n1. Addition:\\n   $$\\n   (\\\\omega_1+\\\\omega_2)(v)=\\\\omega_1(v)+\\\\omega_2(v);\\n   $$\\n\\n2. Scalar multiplication:\\n   $$\\n   (c \\\\cdot \\\\omega)(v)=c\\\\cdot \\\\omega(v).\\n   $$\\n\\nUnder these operations, the set of all dual vectors forms a vector space, denoted by $V^*$.\\n\\n### Dual Basis\\n\\nSince dual vectors form a vector space, we naturally ask for its basis.\\n\\nSuppose $\\\\{e_\\\\mu\\\\}$ is a basis of $V$. We define the **dual basis** as follows.\\n\\n::::tip[Dual basis]\\n\\nGiven a basis $\\\\{e_\\\\mu\\\\}$ of $V$, define $\\\\{e^\\\\mu\\\\}$ in $V^*$ by\\n\\n$$\\ne^\\\\mu(e_\\\\nu)=\\\\delta^\\\\mu{}_\\\\nu.\\n$$\\n\\n:::note\\n\\nHere $\\\\delta^\\\\mu{}_\\\\nu$ is the well-known Kronecker delta:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\delta^\\\\mu{}_\\\\nu=\\n\\\\begin{cases}\\n1,&\\\\mu=\\\\nu,\\\\\\\\\\n0,&\\\\mu\\\\neq\\\\nu.\\n\\\\end{cases}\\n$$\\n\\nIn differential geometry it is written with one upper and one lower index, which makes it behave naturally as the identity matrix.\\n\\nIt satisfies\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\delta^\\\\mu{}_\\\\nu v^\\\\nu=v^\\\\mu.\\n$$\\n\\nThat is, it effectively replaces indices.\\n\\n:::\\n\\n:::note\\n\\nOne should verify that $\\\\{e^\\\\mu\\\\}$ is indeed a basis, meaning that any $\\\\omega\\\\in V^*$ can be expanded as\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\omega=\\\\omega_\\\\mu e^\\\\mu.\\n$$\\n\\nAgain we are using Einstein summation convention.\\n\\nNotice that vector components carry upper indices, while dual vector components carry lower indices.\\n\\nThis is not decorative notation. It reflects different transformation laws, which we will later derive.\\n\\n:::\\n\\n::::\\n\\nFrom this definition, one immediately obtains an important fact:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\dim V=\\\\dim V^*\\n$$\\n\\nfor finite-dimensional vector spaces.\\n\\n:::note\\n\\nMany linear algebra textbooks write the dual basis as $e_\\\\mu^*$.\\n\\nDifferential geometry usually writes it simply as $e^\\\\mu$, which is more convenient once tensors are introduced.\\n\\n:::\\n\\n## The Dual of the Dual: Natural Isomorphism\\n\\nSince $V^*$ is itself a vector space, we can define its dual space $V^{**}$.\\n\\nThis process can clearly be repeated indefinitely, which naturally raises suspicion: do we really need to keep dualizing forever?\\n\\nFortunately, there is a canonical relation between $V$ and $V^{**}$.\\n\\n:::tip[Natural isomorphism between $V$ and $V^{**}$]\\n\\nFor any $v\\\\in V$, define a linear functional on $V^*$ by\\n\\n$$\\nv\'(\\\\omega)=\\\\omega(v).\\n$$\\n\\nBut linear functionals on $V^*$ are precisely elements of $V^{**}$.\\n\\nTherefore every vector in $V$ naturally determines an element of $V^{**}$.\\n\\nIn other words,\\n\\n$$\\nV\\\\cong V^{**}.\\n$$\\n\\nThis isomorphism does not depend on any choice of basis, so it is called a **natural isomorphism** or **canonical isomorphism**.\\n\\n:::\\n\\nThis allows vectors and dual vectors to pair naturally:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\omega(v)=\\\\omega_\\\\mu v^\\\\mu.\\n$$\\n\\nSimilarly,\\n\\n$$\\nv(\\\\omega)=v^\\\\mu\\\\omega_\\\\mu.\\n$$\\n\\nHence,\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\omega_\\\\mu v^\\\\mu=v^\\\\mu\\\\omega_\\\\mu.\\n$$\\n\\nThis may look trivial, but it illustrates an important principle:\\n\\nas long as index structure is correct, components may be manipulated like ordinary scalars, without constantly worrying about compositions of maps.\\n\\nThis is one reason why Einstein summation notation is so powerful.\\n\\n:::note\\n\\nIf the equations above seem mysterious, write the bases explicitly and examine the interaction between basis vectors and dual basis vectors.\\n\\nRemember that components are ordinary real numbers, while dual vectors are linear maps.\\n\\n:::\\n\\n:::warning\\n\\nAll discussion above assumes finite-dimensional vector spaces.\\n\\nFor infinite-dimensional spaces, $V^*$ is typically larger than $V$, and $V^{**}$ is usually larger than $V$ as well.\\n\\nThus in general,\\n\\n$$\\nV\\\\not\\\\cong V^{**}.\\n$$\\n\\nThis is one reason why quantum mechanics, despite involving vectors (kets), dual vectors (bras), and tensors (operators), usually does not directly use Einstein summation convention.\\n\\nIn particular, one must be careful with states such as $|x\\\\rangle$ and $|p\\\\rangle$.\\n\\nInterested readers may look into *rigged Hilbert spaces*.\\n\\n:::"},{"id":"/Differential Geometry and Fibre Bundle/1_1","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/Differential Geometry and Fibre Bundle/1_1","source":"@site/blog/Differential Geometry and Fibre Bundle/1_1.mdx","title":"Basic Topology for Differential Geometry","description":"It\'s assumed that you have basic knowledge of sets, including the definition, Cartesian product, etc.","date":"2026-05-15T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Differential Geometry","permalink":"/blog/tags/differential-geometry"},{"inline":true,"label":"Topology","permalink":"/blog/tags/topology"}],"readingTime":4.87,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Basic Topology for Differential Geometry","date":"2026-05-15T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Differential Geometry","Topology"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Linear Algebra for Differential Geometry","permalink":"/blog/Differential Geometry and Fibre Bundle/1_2"}},"content":"> It\'s assumed that you have basic knowledge of sets, including the definition, Cartesian product, etc.\\n>\\n> In fact, the majority of differential geometry does not require much of the knowledge of topology, at least not the basic definitions. It\'s suggested that beginners should skip this chapter and read the followings directly.\\n\\n## Definition: Topology\\n\\nWhen mathematicians and physicists refer to \\"topology\\", there are some subtle differences involved. Here we focus on the rigorous definition of topology:\\n\\n:::tip[Topology]\\n\\nA set $\\\\mathscr{T}$ is called the **topology** of a set $X$, if $\\\\mathscr{T}$ contains all of the \\"open subsets\\" of $X$. \\n\\n:::\\n\\n:::info[Conditions for topology]\\n\\nTo assure the definition is self-consistent (**all** of the open subsets are contained), $\\\\mathscr{T}$ should satisfy the following conditions:\\n \\n1. $X, \\\\varnothing \\\\in \\\\mathscr{T}$;\\n2. if $\\\\ O_i \\\\in \\\\mathscr{T},  i = 1,  2,  \\\\dots,  n$, then $\\\\bigcap\\\\limits_{i=1}^n O_i \\\\in \\\\mathscr{T}$;\\n3. if$\\\\ O_i \\\\in \\\\mathscr{T},  \\\\forall i$, then $\\\\bigcup\\\\limits_{i} O_i \\\\in \\\\mathscr{T}$. \\n\\n:::\\n\\n:::note\\n\\nNotice that condition 2 require $n$ to be a finite integer, while condition 3 does not. This means only finite intersections remains open, while the union of open sets remains open, even if we take infinite sets. For example, consider: $U_n = \\\\{x | \\\\left|x\\\\right| < 1+ \\\\frac{1}{n} \\\\}$, then $\\\\bigcap\\\\limits_{i=1}^\\\\infty U_n \\\\to [-1,  1]$, not a open set of $\\\\mathbb{R}$!\\n\\n:::\\n\\n## Examples of topology\\n\\nHere we offer three examples of different types of topology. In practice for a given set $X$, there are usually infinitely many ways to specify a topology.\\n\\n:::info[Usual topology]\\n\\nSuppose $X = \\\\mathbb{R}^n$, we say that $\\\\mathscr{T}_u = \\\\{\\\\varnothing, \\\\bigcup_{x_0, r} B(x_0, r)\\\\}$ is the **usual topology** of $\\\\mathbb{R}^n$, where $B(x_0, r) = \\\\{x \\\\in \\\\mathbb{R}^n| \\\\left|x-x_0\\\\right| < r\\\\}$ for $x_0 \\\\in \\\\mathbb{R}^n, r>0 \\\\in \\\\mathbb{R}$ is called the open ball. \\n\\nNotice that the distance in $\\\\mathbb{R}^n$ is defined as: $\\\\left|x-x_0\\\\right| = \\\\sqrt{\\\\sum_{\\\\mu=1}^n(x^\\\\mu-x_0^\\\\mu)^2}$. Here, rather than the exponent, $x^\\\\mu$ means the $\\\\mu$-th component of $x$, this is the common agreement in differential geometry.\\n\\nIn fact, this is the direct generalization of an open interval in $\\\\mathbb{R}$.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info[Discrete topology]\\n\\nFor an arbitrary set $X$, we define $\\\\mathscr{T}$ to be the set containing all subsets of $X$. This is a boring topology.\\n\\n:::\\n\\n:::info[Iniscrete topology]\\n\\nFor an arbitrary set $X$, we define $\\\\mathscr{T} = \\\\{\\\\varnothing, X\\\\}$. This is again a boring topology.\\n\\n:::\\n\\n## Definition: Continuity\\n\\nFor a function $f(x)$ in the usual sense (defined on $\\\\mathbb{R}$), we define its continuity using the $\\\\varepsilon - \\\\delta$ language: \\n\\n:::note[Continuity] \\n\\nIf $\\\\forall \\\\varepsilon > 0$, $\\\\exists \\\\delta > 0$, such that whenever $\\\\left|f(x) - f(x_0)\\\\right| < \\\\varepsilon$, $\\\\left|x - x_0\\\\right| < \\\\delta$ follows. \\n\\n:::\\n\\nHowever, currently we only have a topological space, on which there is likely no well-defined addition, nor can we define an \\"order\\", thus making it impossible to compare magnitudes. \\nAt the same time, we do not have a \\"metric\\" to measure the \\"distance\\" between two elements. Therefore, we turn to seek ways to expand the definition of continuity. \\nFor topological spaces, we only have the means to measure open sets, so we need to rephrase the $\\\\varepsilon - \\\\delta$ language in the language of sets: \\n\\n:::tip[Continuity: Set Language] \\n\\nIf for any open subset $U$ of the image $f[X]$ of the mapping $f$, its preimage $f^{-1}[U]$ is an open subset, then $f$ is continuous. \\n\\n:::\\n\\nAt first glance, this definition seems to make no sense at all, but we can indeed prove that it is equivalent to the $\\\\varepsilon - \\\\delta$ language on $\\\\mathbb{R}$ (in fact, it holds for any topological space equipped with a metric). \\nWe will skip the proof of this equivalence, as it is not necessary for physicists to pay too much attention on the details. However, the proof is quite simple (try using the definition of an open ball). \\n\\nFor a general function, we use $C^r$ to denote that all derivatives up to the $r$-th order exist and are continuous. In particular, $C^0$ refers to continuous functions and $C^\\\\infty$ to smooth functions. \\nWe will adopt this notation for more general mappings as well. Note that for sets with only a topological structure, the highest requirement for mappings defined on them is $C^0$. \\n\\n## Definition: Homeomorphism \\n\\nA homeomorphism is a mapping used to measure how similar two topological spaces are. Specifically, we define: \\n\\n:::tip[Homeomorphism] \\n\\nIf there exists a mapping $f$ between two topological spaces $(X, \\\\mathscr{T})$ and $(Y, \\\\mathscr{S})$ that satisfies the following conditions: \\n1. $f$ is a bijection;\\n2. Both $f$ and $f^{-1}$ are continuous mappings. \\nThen these two topological spaces are said to be homeomorphic to each other, and $f$ is a **homeomorphism** mapping. \\n\\n:::\\n\\n:::note[Note] \\nGenerally speaking, if $f$ is a continuous mapping and its inverse exists, then its inverse mapping is also continuous. However, you can force a counterexample by using the discrete topology and the trivial topology mentioned earlier: \\n\\nLet the topology of $(X, \\\\mathscr{T})$ be the discrete topology (where all subsets are open subsets), and the topology of $(Y, \\\\mathscr{S})$ be the indiscrete topology (where only $Y$ and $\\\\varnothing$ are open subsets). Then, for any bijection $f$, it is continuous: only $Y = f[X]$ is an open subset, and its preimage $f^{-1}[Y] = X$ is also an open set; however, for any inverse mapping $f^{-1}$, it is not continuous: any subset $U \\\\subseteq X$ is an open subset, but its preimage $f^{-1}[U]$ is not an open subset, unless $U = X$. \\n\\nThis is almost the only application of discrete topology and coherent topology, and it is as boring as their definitions. \\n:::"}]}}')}}]);